   221	## 3. The recorded Weyl criterion and the finite-group boundary
   222	
   223	### ⟨1⟩3. COROLLARY SG-WEYL-PAIR (conditional source of kink pairs)
   224	
   225	**ASSUME.**  Let `n_w` represent an ambient Weyl element normalizing a torus,
   226	let `β=n_w·α≠α`, and let an unbroken central direction `ξ` obey
   227	`Ad_{n_w^{-1}}ξ=-ξ`.  Assume `d_α(ξ)=s>0`.
   228	
   229	**PROVE.**  The same circle is unbroken on both tails, its densities are
   230	`(+s,-s)`, and A2 supplies the corresponding fixed kink sector.  Thus this
   231	Weyl-flip criterion supplies an H-MQG(1)--(2) vacuum pair; it does not supply
   232	H-AD-G or D27.
   233	
   234	**⟨2⟩1.**  Covariance of the vacuum family gives
   235	
   236	\[
   237	 d_β(ξ)=d_α(\operatorname{Ad}_{n_w^{-1}}ξ)=-d_α(ξ)=-s .    \tag{SG.7}
   238	\]
   239	
   240	*Justification.*  D2(a) gives
   241	`ω_{g·α}(C_ξ)=ω_α(C_{Ad_{g^{-1}}ξ})`; the exact fixed-point obstruction and
   242	the Weyl-flip realization are already recorded in claim M-quant-G and
   243	`memory-quantization-general.md` §3.
   244	
   245	**⟨2⟩2.**  Since `ξ∈Lie Z(H_α)` and `Ad_{n_w^{-1}}ξ=-ξ`, the conjugate
   246	stabilizer `H_β=n_wH_αn_w^{-1}` also contains the same circle; hence it is a
   247	common unbroken direction.
   248	
   249	*Justification.*  Stabilizers are D2(a)'s `H_γ`; Weyl conjugation of root and
   250	torus rows is the recorded criterion (H.28) in `soft-index-general.md`, also
   251	summarized by claim S-IDX-fin-G.
   252	
   253	**⟨2⟩3.**  For `β≠α`, A2(a,c,d,f) supplies and superselects
   254	`𝒦_{αβ}`.  No channel or relaxation hypothesis follows from this group
   255	construction.
   256	
   257	*Justification.*  Claim A2 and D9(a,b); the non-implication to H-AD-G is the
   258	explicit status fence in claim M-quant-G, and the non-implication to D27 is
   259	the explicit fence in claim M-INDEX-spec.  **⟨2⟩4. QED.**
   260	
   261	**Existence fence.**  A Weyl element supplies a calibrated pair only on a
   262	direction in its `-1` eigenspace and only when it moves `α` to a distinct
   263	vacuum.  There is no assertion that every compact group, every central
   264	circle, or every Weyl orbit has such an element.  Directions fixed by the
   265	element have equal tail densities and are spectators, as in M-quant-G's
   266	rank-two semidirect-product example.
   267	
   268	*Justification.*  Equation (SG.7) and claim M-quant-G; its §3 checker records
   269	one flipped and one fixed circle.
   270	
   271	### ⟨1⟩4. COROLLARY SG-NO-CALIBRATION
   272	
   273	**ASSUME.**  Either (i) the selected unbroken direction lies in the
   274	semisimple summand of `𝔥_α`, or (ii) the compact on-site symmetry is finite.
   275	
   276	**PROVE.**  Case (i) has zero vacuum density and cannot furnish D13(a)'s
   277	nonzero calibration.  Case (ii) has no Lie-algebra circle, D10 current, or
   278	real D13 displacement calibration.  A finite torsion character or string
   279	endpoint may remain, but it is a different observable.
   280	
   281	**⟨2⟩1.**  In case (i), `d_α(ξ)=0`, so no positive `s` can satisfy
   282	H-MQG(2) for that direction.
   283	
   284	*Justification.*  SG-CENTRE ⟨1⟩1 and D13(a)/H-MQG(2).
   285	
   286	**⟨2⟩2.**  In case (ii), `Lie(G)=0`; hence D10 has no nonzero `q(ξ)` or cut
   287	current to integrate and D26 has no circle coordinate to calibrate.
   288	
   289	*Justification.*  D10 and D26; claims S-IDX-fin-G and S-IDX-G-label explicitly
   290	record that finite groups have no Lie-current instance, while the latter
   291	allows only a finite torsion label.
   292	
   293	**⟨2⟩3.**  Finite strings can instead leave exact endpoint operators, but
   294	that group-valued endpoint statement is not an additive memory law.
   295	
   296	*Justification.*  The finite-group exclusion and endpoint replacement are
   297	recorded by `soft-index-g-boundary.md` G5-C2-FINITE and claim S-IDX-G-label.
   298	**⟨2⟩4. QED.**
   299	
   300	## 4. Scope theorem
   301	
   302	### ⟨1⟩5. THEOREM SG-SCOPE
   303	
   304	**ASSUME.**  A compact on-site symmetry `G` and the hypotheses of the relevant
   305	parts above.
   306	
   307	**PROVE.**  Every vacuum-density calibration functional factors through the
   308	connected center of the common unbroken subgroup, and its nonzero density
   309	jump descends through the scalar kernel to the connected effective center.
   310	Canonical independent calibrated coordinates may therefore be chosen on that
   311	effective central torus.  Their finite TPM
   312	charge outcome is a weight-lattice element, equivalently an integer vector
   313	after choosing primitive circles, and D13 converts that vector one active
   314	circle at a time to the outcome `-ν_j/(2s_j)`.  Under the joint D27 hypothesis
   315	the ordered law and its component means obey (SG.3).  A Weyl representative
   316	obeying SG-WEYL-PAIR supplies a compatible opposite-density kink pair.
   317	Semisimple unbroken directions and finite groups supply no calibrated memory.
   318	
   319	**⟨2⟩1.**  Each tail density is supported on the group center.  A direction
   320	whose on-site action is scalar has the same density in every state, so every
   321	nonzero tail-density jump descends to the effective center.
   322	
   323	*Justification.*  SG-CENTRE ⟨1⟩1; D2(a) makes a scalar on-site generator
   324	state-independent, and claim S-IDX-G-label records the scalar-kernel quotient.
   325	
   326	**⟨2⟩2.**  The finite and ordered joint outcome statements are respectively
   327	the two conclusions of SG-TORUS-TPM.
   328	
   329	*Justification.*  SG-TORUS-TPM ⟨1⟩2, claims M-INDEX-fin and M-INDEX-spec,
   330	and D13/D26/D27.
   331	
   332	**⟨2⟩3.**  Compatible flipped pairs and the two exclusions have exactly the
   333	scope stated in the theorem.
   334	
   335	*Justification.*  SG-WEYL-PAIR ⟨1⟩3 and SG-NO-CALIBRATION ⟨1⟩4, whose
   336	load-bearing inputs are D2, D9, D10, D13, D26 and claims A2,
   337	S-IDX-fin-G, and S-IDX-G-label.
   338	**⟨2⟩4. QED.**
   339	
   340	**Interpretation fence.**  “For any compact `G`” classifies the only possible
   341	location and lattice of a memory effect; it does not assert that every `G`
   342	has an active center, a flipping Weyl element, a kink, scattering channels,
   343	or relaxation.  An ambient semisimple group may still exhibit memory after
   344	breaking to a subgroup with a central circle (for example `SU(2)→U(1)`); what
   345	vanishes is density along the semisimple part of the **unbroken** algebra.
   346	
   347	*Justification.*  D2(a), claim M-quant-G's `SU(2)→U(1)` example and
   348	conditionality fence, and SG-CENTRE.
   349	
   350	## 5. Checker and red capability
   351	
   352	Run:
   353	
   354	```text
   355	python3 -O theory/lanes/blitz-2026-08-29/scope-g/scope_g_check.py
   356	python3 -O theory/lanes/blitz-2026-08-29/scope-g/scope_g_check.py --red
   357	```
   358	
   359	Named computation **SG-C1** makes 24 deterministic random covariant
   360	`χ=1` MPS tensors and 24 invariant density-tensor samples for nonabelian
   361	`H=U(1)×SU(2)`, all after random basis changes.  The MPS tensors are random
   362	vectors in a fourfold singlet multiplicity inside a representation that also
   363	contains a nontrivial spin-one multiplet; as nonzero `χ=1` tensors they are
   364	injective by construction.  The gate checks their covariance, the central
   365	character, the `su(2)` brackets, state
   366	covariance, `Ad(H)` invariance, zero density on all three semisimple
   367	generators, and a permitted nonzero central density.  The red mutation adds a
   368	highest-weight triplet component to each MPS tensor and also biases the
   369	positive density tensor by `J_z`; covariance breaks and semisimple density is
   370	nonzero.
   371	
   372	Named computation **SG-C2** constructs three commuting on-site Cartan charges,
   373	two random same-time joint eigenbases with noncommuting cross-time charge
   374	observables, and the full 625-atom TPM law.  It checks normalization and
   375	affine-offset cancellation into `ℤ^3`.  The red mutation changes the final
   376	background by `√2/10` and the lattice gate fails.  Recorded outputs are
   377	`scope_g_check.out` (exit `0`) and `scope_g_check.red.out` (expected exit `1`).
   378	These finite checks test the algebraic premises and mutation sensitivity; they
   379	do not prove D27, H-AD-G, or existence of a Weyl-flipped vacuum pair.
   380	
   381	*Justification.*  Named computations `scope_g_check.py` **SG-C1--SG-C2**;
   382	their scope matches D2/D10 and M-INDEX-fin, not D18 or D27.

