===== ^## D2  =====
   132	## D2 (on-site symmetry; covariant vacuum family; intertwining relation)
   133	
   134	Let `G` be a compact group (finite or a compact Lie group) with a unitary
   135	representation `u: G → U(ℂ^d)` on one site, and let `U_Λ(g) := ⨂_{x∈Λ} u_x(g)`.
   136	Write `𝒰(g)` for the induced linear map on tensors,
   137	`(𝒰(g)A)^s := Σ_{s'} u(g)_{s s'} A^{s'}`, `u(g)_{ss'} := ⟨s|u(g)|s'⟩`;
   138	`𝒰(g)𝒰(h) = 𝒰(gh)`.
   139	
   140	**(a) Covariant vacuum family.** A *`G`-covariant vacuum family* is a finite or
   141	compact set `Ω_vac` of labels, injective canonical-form tensors `{A_α}_{α∈Ω_vac}`
   142	of common bond dimension `χ`, generating pairwise distinct states `ω_α := ω_{A_α}`,
   143	together with a `G`-action `(g,α) ↦ g·α` on `Ω_vac` such that
   144	`U_Λ(g)` maps `ω_α` to `ω_{g·α}` for every finite `Λ`, in the sense
   145	`ω_α ∘ Ad(U_Λ(g)^†) = ω_{g·α}` on `𝔄_Λ` for all `Λ`.
   146	`H_α := { g ∈ G : g·α = α }` is the *stabiliser* (the *unbroken subgroup*).
   147	The family is *unbroken at `α`* if `H_α = G` (then `|Ω_vac|` may be `1`).
   148	
   149	**(b) Intertwining relation (IT).** For every `α ∈ Ω_vac` and `g ∈ G` there
   150	exist a phase `θ_α(g) ∈ ℝ/2πℤ` and `V_α(g) ∈ U(χ)` with
   151	
   152	  `Σ_{s'} u(g)_{s s'} A_α^{s'} = e^{i θ_α(g)} V_α(g)^{-1} A_{g·α}^{s} V_α(g)`  for all `s`.   **(IT)**
   153	
   154	The pair `(θ_α(g), V_α(g))` is unique up to `V_α(g) ↦ e^{iφ} V_α(g)`.
   155	*Ground truth:* this is the fundamental theorem of MPS.  For the existence,
   156	refs/arxiv-0802.0447 (`StringOrder-v10.tex`), Lemma 1: `ρ(E_u) ≤ 1` with
   157	equality iff there are a unitary `V` and `θ` with
   158	`V^† Ã_j = e^{i(θ−θ_j)} Ã_j V^†`, together with condition C1
   159	`(u⊗𝟙)B = (𝟙⊗V) B V^†`; and refs/arxiv-2011.12127 (`TN-Review-main.tex`),
   160	which for `U(g)^{⊗N}|ψ_N⟩ ≃ |ψ_N⟩` states
   161	`Σ_j U_{ij}(g) A^j = e^{iφ(g)} X^†(g) A^i X(g)`.
   162	For the uniqueness, refs/arxiv-2011.12127 Eq. (eq:XAX=B):
   163	`X^{-1}A^i X = e^{iχ} Y^{-1} A^i Y ⟹ χ = 0 ∧ ∃φ: X = e^{iφ}Y`.
   164	
   165	**(c) Cocycles.** Composition of (IT) (proved in corner-a.md ⟨1⟩2) gives, for
   166	all `g,h ∈ G`,
   167	  `θ_α(hg) = θ_α(g) + θ_{g·α}(h)`,   `V_{g·α}(h) V_α(g) = e^{i ω_α(h,g)} V_α(hg)`.
   168	Restricted to `g,h ∈ H_α` these say that `θ_α|_{H_α}: H_α → U(1)` is a
   169	homomorphism and that `ω_α: H_α × H_α → U(1)` is a 2-cocycle.  Its class
   170	`[ω_α] ∈ H²(H_α, U(1))` is the *SPT index* of `A_α`.  (Notation hazard: `ω`
   171	with two group arguments is this cocycle; `ω(k)` with a momentum argument is
   172	the magnon dispersion of D6.  The two never occur with the same argument type.)
   173	
   174	**(d) Normal ordering.** `ǔ_α(g) := e^{−iθ_α(g)} u(g)` for `g ∈ H_α` is again a
   175	unitary representation of `H_α` (because `θ_α|_{H_α}` is a homomorphism), and
   176	satisfies (IT) with phase `1`.  All charges below are those of `ǔ_α` unless
   177	stated otherwise; physically this is the charge measured from its vacuum value.
   178	
   179	**(e) Smoothness hypothesis (S).** When `G` is a Lie group we assume, and state
   180	whenever used, that `ε ↦ A_{exp(εξ)·α}` and `ε ↦ V_α(exp(εξ))` admit
   181	`C¹` local sections with `V_α(e) = 𝟙`.  We then write
   182	`X_α(ξ) := d/dε V_α(exp(εξ))|_{ε=0}` (anti-Hermitian) and
   183	`θ'_α(ξ) := d/dε θ_α(exp(εξ))|_{ε=0}`.
   184	
   185	---
   186	
   187	## D3 (admissible profiles; truncated and modulated symmetry operations)
   188	
   189	**(a) Profile classes (the function-space discipline).** Let
   190	`𝔉_c := { f: ℤ → G : f(x) = e for all but finitely many x }`,
   191	`𝔉_ec := { f: ℤ → G : f is constant on some (−∞,−n] and on some [n,∞) }`,
   192	and for a Lie algebra element `ξ ∈ 𝔤` let
   193	`𝔉_c(ξ) := { f: ℤ → ℝ finitely supported }`,
   194	`𝔉_ec(ξ) := { f: ℤ → ℝ : f constant near ±∞ }`,
   195	`𝔉_{ℓ¹}(ξ) := { f: ℤ → ℝ : Σ_x |f(x+1) − f(x)| < ∞ }`  (finite total variation).
   196	`𝔉_c ⊂ 𝔉_ec` and `𝔉_ec(ξ) ⊂ 𝔉_{ℓ¹}(ξ)`.  A profile is *admissible* for an
   197	operator statement iff it lies in `𝔉_c` (then the operator is in `𝔄_loc`); it
   198	is admissible for a *state* statement iff it lies in `𝔉_ec` (then the operator
   199	is a weak-* limit of elements of `𝔄_loc`, cf. A1(c), A2).  The plane wave
   200	`f(x) = e^{ikx}` lies in none of these for `k ≠ 0`; it is admitted only inside
   201	a Fourier transform, i.e. as the distributional kernel of a wave packet
   202	`Σ_k φ(k) f_k` with `φ ∈ C_c^∞((−π,π])`.  Every soft statement must say which
   203	class it uses.
   204	
   205	**(b) Truncated symmetry.** For finite `R = [a,b] ⊂ ℤ`,
   206	`U_R(g) := Π_{x∈R} u_x(g) ∈ 𝔄_loc`.  Its *boundary bonds* are
   207	`∂_-R := (a−1|a)` and `∂_+R := (b|b+1)`; `|R| = b−a+1`.
   208	More generally for `f ∈ 𝔉_c`, `U[f] := Π_x u_x(f(x)) ∈ 𝔄_loc`.
   209	
   210	**(c) Half-infinite strings.** For `f ∈ 𝔉_ec` the product `U[f]` is *not* an
   211	element of `𝔄`.  It is used only through the induced maps on states:
   212	`ϱ ↦ w*-lim_{n→∞} ϱ ∘ Ad(U[f_n]^†)`, where `f_n ∈ 𝔉_c` agrees with `f` on
   213	`[−n,n]` and is `e` outside.  Existence of this limit is a theorem, not a
   214	definition (A1(c), A2); when it exists the limit state is written `f ⊳ ϱ`.
   215	The canonical case is `f = 1_{[x,∞)}·g`, written `U_{[x,∞)}(g)`.
   216	
   217	---
   218	
   219	## D4 (bond implementers; effective asymptotic symmetry; charge algebra)
   220	
   221	Fix a bond `b`, a vacuum label `α`, and work in the unbroken case `H_α = G`
   222	(the broken case is D9/A2).  *(Rewritten in r2: the r1 version used the
   223	implementer `V_α(g)^{-1}` with left multiplication, which reverses the
   224	composition law, and asserted a twisted-algebra action on states, where phases
   225	are invisible.  See corner-a.md ⟨1⟩4 and the r1 objections 3, 4, 7, 8.)*
   226	
   227	**(a) Bond implementers act on *padded* window vectors.**

===== ^## D10  =====
   466	## D10 (lattice Noether pair: charge density, current, modulated charge)
   467	
   468	Let `H = Σ_x h_{x,x+1}` be a translation-invariant nearest-neighbour
   469	Hamiltonian (the definition extends verbatim to finite range `R_h`), let
   470	`𝔤 = Lie(G)`, and for `ξ ∈ 𝔤` let `q(ξ) := d/dε u(exp(εξ))|_{ε=0}`
   471	(anti-Hermitian on `ℂ^d`), with `q_x(ξ)` its copy at site `x`.  Assume `H` is
   472	`G`-invariant on-site: `[h_{x,x+1}, q_x(ξ) + q_{x+1}(ξ)] = 0`.
   473	
   474	**(a) Cut current.** For *finite range* `R_h` (each `h_x` supported in
   475	`[x, x+R_h−1]`) and `G`-invariance `[h_x, Σ_{y∈supp h_x} q_y(ξ)] = 0`, define the
   476	current across the bond `(m|m+1)` as
   477	  `j_{m|m+1}(ξ) := −[H, Σ_{y ≤ m} q_y(ξ)] = −Σ_x [h_x, Σ_{y≤m} q_y(ξ)]`.
   478	The sum over `x` is finite — at most `R_h` terms, those whose support straddles
   479	the cut — because `[h_x, Σ_{y≤m}q_y] = 0` when `supp h_x` lies wholly on one
   480	side.  Hence `j_{m|m+1}(ξ) ∈ 𝔄_loc`.  For the nearest-neighbour case `R_h = 2`
   481	this reduces to `j_{x|x+1}(ξ) = −[h_{x,x+1}, q_x(ξ)] = [h_{x,x+1}, q_{x+1}(ξ)]`.
   482	*(r2: the r1 definition was the nearest-neighbour formula while (H4) quantified
   483	over finite range — objection 19.)*
   484	
   485	**(b) Continuity equation.** `[H, q_x(ξ)] = j_{x−1|x}(ξ) − j_{x|x+1}(ξ)`
   486	(proved in corner-a-goldstone.md ⟨1⟩6); this is the exact lattice conservation law.
   487	
   488	**(c) Modulated charge and current.** For `f ∈ 𝔉_c(ξ)`,
   489	`Q[f;ξ] := Σ_x f(x) q_x(ξ) ∈ 𝔄_loc` and `J[f;ξ] := Σ_x f(x) j_{x|x+1}(ξ)`.
   490	For `f(x) = e^{ikx}` we write `Q_k(ξ)`, `J_k(ξ)`, understood in the
   491	wave-packet sense of D3(a).
   492	
   493	**(d) Bond current potential (the "potential" of docs/framing.md).** In the
   494	unbroken case with (IT) and normal ordering (D2(d)), `𝒥_b(ξ)` denotes the
   495	operation "insert `X_α(ξ)` on bond `b`" (D4(a)).  Theorem G0(d)
   496	(corner-a-goldstone.md ⟨1⟩6) states `q_x(ξ) ▹ ω_α = (𝒥_{x|x+1}(ξ) − 𝒥_{x-1|x}(ξ)) ▹ ω_α`: the
   497	physical charge density acting on the vacuum is the lattice divergence of a
   498	purely virtual (bond) quantity.  `𝒥` is the lattice analogue of the gauge
   499	potential; `q` is the field strength.
   500	
   501	---
   502	
   503	## D11 (Goldstone tensor; broken and unbroken directions)
   504	
   505	Assume (S) of D2(e).  Fix `α ∈ Ω_vac`, write `𝔥_α := Lie(H_α)`, and choose an
   506	`Ad(H_α)`-invariant complement `𝔪_α` with `𝔤 = 𝔥_α ⊕ 𝔪_α` (the *broken
   507	directions*).
   508	
   509	**(a) Goldstone tensor.** `B_G(ξ) := d/dε [ 𝒰(exp(εξ)) A_α ]|_{ε=0}` for
   510	`ξ ∈ 𝔤`.  Equivalently `B_G(ξ)^s = Σ_{s'} q(ξ)_{s s'} A_α^{s'}`.
   511	
   512	**(b) Soft insertion.** `|Φ_k(B_G(ξ))⟩ = Q_k(ξ) ▹ ω_α` in the sense of D5(a)
   513	and D10(c): replacing `A_α` by `B_G(ξ)` at site `n` is exactly the action of
   514	`q_n(ξ)`.
   515	
   516	**(c) Goldstone count.** The number of independent Goldstone tensors at `α` is
   517	`dim_ℂ span_ℂ{ B_G(ξ) : ξ ∈ 𝔪_α } mod 𝒩_0(·)`, which may be strictly smaller
   518	than `dim_ℝ 𝔪_α`.  The deficiency is the *type-B* phenomenon; the isotropic
   519	ferromagnet realises `dim_ℝ 𝔪_α = 2 → 1` Goldstone mode (corner-a-goldstone.md ⟨1⟩7).
   520	
   521	---
   522	
   523	## D12 (limits in which the ansatz gauge remainder vanishes)
   524	
   525	*(Rewritten in r3.  The r2 version over-quantified in three ways — r2
   526	objection 4 — all conceded: (i) it asserted that the identity "holds exactly"
   527	for every `f ∈ c_0`, but `c_0 ⊄ ℓ¹` and neither side need converge in norm
   528	(e.g. `f(n) = (1+|n|)^{-1/4}`); (ii) it asserted a universal
   529	`Θ(|Λ|^{1/2})` bulk growth, which is **false** — for `χ = 1`, `A_αX ∝ A_α`, so
   530	the plane-wave bulk sum is a bounded geometric sum (check **C10**); (iii) it
   531	attached a fixed-`k` formula to a `c_0` hypothesis, though a plane wave is not
   532	in `c_0`.  D12 now states only what is proved: a bound on the **remainder**.)*
   533	
   534	Let `Λ_L := [−L,L]` and `|Φ_f^{Λ}(B)⟩ := Σ_{n∈Λ} f(n)|ψ_Λ(…B@n…)⟩`.  Lemma SBP
   535	(corner-a-goldstone.md ⟨1⟩5) shows the gauge remainder is a sum of exactly two
   536	boundary window vectors and gives the **upper bound**
   537	  `‖𝔅_Λ[f,X]‖ ≤ 2C_∂‖X‖\,\max(|f(a)|,|f(b)|)`   (D1(e)).
   538	Everything below is a consequence of this bound alone.  Three regimes are used;
   539	every statement citing D12 must name which.
   540	
   541	**(a) Vanishing remainder (decaying profiles).** If `f ∈ c_0(ℤ)` then
   542	`‖𝔅_Λ[f,X]‖ → 0` as `Λ_L ↗ ℤ`.  **This is a statement about the remainder
   543	only.**  It does *not* assert that either side of the SBP identity converges to
   544	a vector: for that one needs a summability class (see (a′)) *and* the split
   545	property of corner-a.md ⟨1⟩4.⟨2⟩9, which is SKETCH.  Measured decay with the
   546	centred profile `(1+|n−c|)^{-3}`: `‖𝔅_Λ‖/‖bulk‖ = 3.4·10^{-1}, 5.7·10^{-2},
   547	8.5·10^{-3}, 1.2·10^{-3}` for `L = 4,8,16,32` (check **C4**).
   548	
   549	**(a′) Norm-convergent wave packets.** If in addition `f ∈ ℓ¹(ℤ)` **and**
   550	`Δf ∈ ℓ¹(ℤ)` (i.e. `f` is summable of bounded variation), then both sides of
   551	the SBP identity are absolutely convergent sums of window vectors of norm
   552	`≤ C_∂‖X‖`, uniformly in `Λ`; the identity then holds between the two limits at
   553	fixed `Λ` and remains remainderless as `Λ ↗ ℤ`.  A smooth compactly supported
   554	momentum packet `f = \hatφ`, `φ ∈ C_c^∞((−π,π])`, has rapidly decreasing `f`
   555	and so lies in `ℓ¹ ∩ BV`.  `𝔉_{ℓ¹}` of D3(a) is exactly the `BV` condition.
   556	**This class, not `c_0`, is the one to cite for wave-packet statements.**
   557	
   558	**(b) δ-normalised plane wave.** For `f(n) = e^{ikn}`, `|f| ≡ 1`, so the bound
   559	gives `‖𝔅_Λ‖ = O(1)`, **uniformly in `Λ`** — and that is all that is needed:
   560	  `‖\,|Λ|^{-1/2}\,𝔅_Λ\,‖ = O(|Λ|^{-1/2}) → 0`.
   561	No claim is made, or needed, about how the bulk term grows; the r2 assertion

===== ^## D13  =====
   587	## D13 (memory observables: windowed wall position, and its two dresses)
   588	
   589	**(a) Windowed wall-position observable — the frozen definition (`Bd3`).**
   590	For a finite window `W = [a,b] ⊂ ℤ` in a chain with `S^z`-vacuum densities
   591	`s_α = +s` at `−∞` and `s_β = −s` at `+∞`, put
   592	
   593	  `𝔛_W := a − 1 + \frac{1}{2s}\sum_{x=a}^{b}(S^z_x + s) ∈ 𝔄_loc`,
   594	
   595	normalised so that `𝔛_W = m` on a sharp wall at bond `(m|m+1)` with no other
   596	content in `W`.  The *spatial memory* of an event running from `t_i` to `t_f`
   597	is `δx := ϱ_{t_f}(𝔛_W) − ϱ_{t_i}(𝔛_W)`.
   598	
   599	`𝔛_W ∈ 𝔄_loc` unconditionally, so `δx` exists on all of `𝔄^*` and at finite
   600	`N`, with no asymptotic hypothesis and no order-of-limits clause.  For `δx` to
   601	*mean* a wall displacement one needs additionally
   602	(i) the packet outside `W` at `t ∈ {t_i,t_f}` up to `ε`, and
   603	(ii) the kink core padded from both edges of `W`.  If `d_W` is the minimum
   604	core-to-edge distance, D1(c) gives, for every `λ̃ ∈ (λ_E,1)`, a tail bound
   605	`C_{λ̃}λ̃^{d_W}`.  A bare `O(e^{-(b-a)/ξ_c}) = O(λ_E^{b-a})` is not valid for
   606	a transfer operator with a Jordan block at modulus `λ_E`.
   607	**This is the definition the campaign freezes**; (b) and (c) are corollary
   608	characterisations, not competing definitions.
   609	
   610	**(b) Spectral dress (`Bd2`).** With `m_x(t) := ϱ_t(S^z_x)` and DC weight
   611	`D(x) := \lim_{ω→0}\int dt\,e^{iωt}\dot m_x(t) = m_x(+∞) − m_x(−∞)`,
   612	`δx^{\rm spec} := \frac{1}{2s}\sum_{x∈W}D(x)`.  Identically equal to (a) — the
   613	same observable in a Fourier dress — and requires `\dot m_x ∈ L¹(dt)` for each
   614	`x ∈ W`.  The `ω→0` limit must be taken at fixed `x` and fixed `W`, *after* the
   615	thermodynamic limit; interchanging `ω→0` with `|W|→∞` destroys it.
   616	
   617	**(c) Dynamical dress (`Bd1`), and its trap.** With the first-moment wall
   618	coordinate
   619	`X_1(t) := \sum_{x>0}\frac{ϱ_t(S^z_x)+s}{2s} − \sum_{x≤0}\frac{s-ϱ_t(S^z_x)}{2s}`
   620	(convergent on D17) and `V_± := \lim_{t→±∞}\dot X_1(t)`,
   621	`δx^{\rm dyn} := \lim_{t→+∞}[X_1(t) − V_+t] − \lim_{t→−∞}[X_1(t) − V_-t]`.
   622	Requires the thermodynamic limit *before* `t → ±∞`, and is meaningless on a
   623	ring.  **Trap:** `X_1` is the regularised total magnetisation, which is exactly
   624	conserved, so `δx^{\rm dyn} ≡ 0` unless the asymptotic leg charges (D14) are
   625	first subtracted — i.e. unless (H-AD), D18, is invoked.  (a) performs that
   626	subtraction geometrically, by windowing, and needs no such hypothesis.
   627	
   628	## D14 (asymptotic leg content: transmitted and reflected magnon number)
   629	
   630	*(`Bd4`.)*  For `ϱ ∈ 𝒦^{(1)}_{αβ}` (D17) and a window `W = [a,b]`,
   631	
   632	  `N_R := \frac{1}{2s}\sum_{x<a}(s − ϱ(S^z_x))`,
   633	  `N_T := \frac{1}{2s}\sum_{x>b}(ϱ(S^z_x) + s)`,
   634	
   635	both convergent on `𝒦^{(1)}`.  For a one-magnon initial state
   636	`N_R + N_T + N_W = 1`, with `N_W` the weight inside `W`.  For a packet `φ`,
   637	`⟨N_T⟩ = \int_{-π}^{π}\frac{dk}{2π}|φ(k)|²T(k)` — a *packet average* of `T`
   638	(D15), **not** `T(⟨k⟩)`; the difference is `\tfrac12 T''(⟨k⟩)σ_k^2` and is
   639	largest exactly in the soft region.
   640	
   641	## D15 (kink--magnon scattering data)
   642	
   643	*(`Bd5`.)*  In `𝒦_{↑↓}` with one magnon present, the stationary scattering
   644	solutions at magnon momentum `k ∈ (0,π)` define `r(k)`, `t(k)` by the
   645	asymptotics: an incoming `↓`-magnon of momentum `k` in the `↑` region goes to
   646	`r(k)·`(`↓`-magnon, momentum `−k`, `↑` region) `+ t(k)·`(`↑`-magnon, momentum
   647	`k`, `↓` region).  Put `T(k) := |t(k)|²`, `R(k) := |r(k)|²` (so `T + R = 1`),
   648	and let `δ_t(k) := \arg t(k)` be the *kink--magnon transmission phase*, fixed
   649	continuous with `δ_t → 0` at the point where `t → 1`.
   650	`dδ_t/dk` is the spatial (Wigner--Eisenbud) shift of the **transmitted
   651	magnon**; it is a smooth, non-quantised function of `k` and `Δ`, and it is
   652	**not** the wall displacement D13(a).
   653	
   654	## D16 (easy-axis XXZ kink model and its conventions)
   655	
   656	*(`Bd6`, together with the draft's §1.1--§1.2.)*  Fix `J > 0` and `Δ > 1`,
   657	and let
   658	
   659	  `h^{XXZ}_{x,x+1} := −J[S^x_xS^x_{x+1} + S^y_xS^y_{x+1} + Δ(S^z_xS^z_{x+1} − \tfrac14)]`,
   660	  `ω(k) = J(Δ − \cos k)`, `v(k) = J\sin k`, `ω_{\rm gap} = J(Δ−1)`.
   661	
   662	`Δ` is the same anisotropy as `notation.md`'s `Δ = J_z/J_⊥` (the two
   663	conventions agree; this fixes the draft's interface item R9).  At `Δ = 1` this
   664	reduces to D6/O1.  Every "soft" statement in this model is a low-frequency
   665	expansion *about the gap*.  `G = U(1) ⋊ ℤ₂` with `U(1)` generated by `S^z`
   666	(unbroken) and `ℤ₂` the `π`-rotation about `S^x` (broken); `Ω_vac = {↑,↓}`,
   667	both exact injective product vacua with `χ = 1`.
   668	
   669	**Kink normalisation.**
   670	`h^{\rm kink}_{x,x+1} := h^{XXZ}_{x,x+1} + \tfrac{J}{2}\sqrt{Δ^2−1}(S^z_x − S^z_{x+1})`,
   671	`H_{\rm kink} := \sum_x h^{\rm kink}_{x,x+1}`.  The added field is a telescoping
   672	boundary term: `H_{\rm kink}` and `H_{XXZ}` generate the **same** derivation on
   673	`𝔄`, so every dynamical statement is a statement about the pure XXZ chain and
   674	`H_{\rm kink}` serves only to normalise the kink to zero energy.
   675	
   676	**Kink coordinates.** `q := Δ − \sqrt{Δ^2−1} ∈ (0,1)`, equivalently
   677	`Δ = (q + q^{-1})/2`; the exact zero-energy product family is
