   769	Fix `φ(y)=c_φe^{-1/(1-y²)}` for `|y|<1` and zero otherwise, with
   770	`∫|φ|²=1`.  The bulk family is
   771	`f_{L,κ}^{bulk}(x)=Z_{L,κ}^{-1/2}φ(x/L)e^{iκx/L}` with
   772	`Z_{L,κ}=Σ_x|φ(x/L)|²`; the edge family is
   773	`f_L^{edge}=1_{[0,L-1]}`.  For a finite group use
   774	`g_L^{edge}(x)=g` on `[0,L-1]` and `e` otherwise, with the normal-ordered
   775	on-site operator `ǔ_C(g)=e^{-iθ_C(g)}u(g)`.  Every finite-`L` object is local.
   776	Limits are ordered: `N→∞` at fixed `L` with `N-L→∞`; normalize packet
   777	states; take scattering/time limits if present; then `L→∞` (`k_L∼L^{-1}→0`).
   778	For finite `G` the last operation is only a large-window endpoint/string limit,
   779	not an infinitesimal group or momentum-soft limit.
   780	
   781	## D20 (operator-valued soft insertions and scalar form factors)
   782	
   783	For fixed normalized finite-dimensional bulk channel registers and embeddings
   784	`W_{in/out}^{N,L}`, define
   785	`𝔉_{N,L}^{bulk}(ξ)=(W_out^{N,L})†Q[f_{L,κ}^{bulk};ξ]W_in^{N,L}`.
   786	Along a tensor path, every external tensor, channel embedding, tangent-space
   787	gauge fix, Gram normalization, and differentiated profile entering this
   788	formula is required to be continuous in the path parameter, or `C^p` when a
   789	coefficient through order `p` is asserted.
   790	
   791	D10 uses the anti-Hermitian charge convention.  Define the Hermitian
   792	modulated charge `Q^{H}[f;ξ]:=-iQ[f;ξ]` and, on the edge transfer register,
   793	define
   794	`ℜ_{C,L}(ξ)=𝒞_C(Q^{H}[f_L^{edge};ξ])` and
   795	`𝕊_{C,L}^{comp}(g)` as the decorated contraction of
   796	`Ǔ_{C,[0,L-1]}(g)` with `V_C(g)^{-1}` on the remote cut, viewed on the dual
   797	left-edge register.  The compensation cancels the far WI endpoint; an
   798	uncompensated string overlap may decay.  Soft residues are operator-norm limits
   799	after the D19 order.  Scalar form factors are only their matrix elements, e.g.
   800	`F_{C,L}^{edge}(e',e;ξ)=⟨e'|ℜ_{C,L}(ξ)|e⟩`; a scalar is never algebra-valued.
   801	
   802	Thermodynamic limits are uniform only on compact tensor paths with a common
   803	transfer bound.  A coefficient through order `p` carries hypothesis (H-soft-p):
   804	the soft limit is uniform through `p` derivatives.  Scattering statements also
   805	carry the required wave-operator/asymptotic-completeness hypotheses.
   806	
   807	## D21 (endpoint module, shifted charge lattice, and physical-edge hypothesis)
   808	
   809	The unconditional Schmidt/edge register is `E_C=ℂ^χ`, carrying the projective
   810	action `V_C(g)` with multiplier `ω_C`.  It is distinct from A1(d1)'s
   811	padded-window matrix module
   812	`𝓜_χ(C):=𝒲_{Λ,b}≅M_χ(ℂ)≅E_C⊗E_C^*`.
   813	Under A1(d1)'s left multiplication, `V_C(g)` acts on the first tensor factor
   814	and the dual factor is a spectator, so `𝓜_χ(C)` is exactly `χ` copies of the
   815	`E_C` projective module.  This multiplicity distinction does not change the
   816	lower bound below.
   817	`d_ω` is the minimal dimension of an `ω`-projective irrep.  A nontrivial class
   818	has `d_ω>1` because a one-dimensional multiplier is a coboundary.
   819	
   820	**(H-split):** the half-chain ground/low-energy subspace has a normal split
   821	realisation and an isometry `J_C:E_C→H_edge` intertwining the **Schmidt/edge
   822	register** action `V_C` with the physical half-chain symmetry, and registered
   823	local contractions converge to the corresponding compressed physical
   824	operators.  H-split is not a statement about all `χ²` padded-window matrices.
   825	This is the explicit hypothesis replacing the SKETCH box `corner-a.md`
   826	⟨1⟩4.⟨2⟩9.
   827	
   828	If the identity component of compact `G` contains a circle, let the universal
   829	cover of that component have kernel `Z`.  Assume a lift normalised compatibly
   830	with the full group and the physical vacuum charge fixes a class-invariant
   831	central character `ν_ω:Z→U(1)`.  If the lifted `2π` path of a
   832	circle generator `ξ` ends at `z_ξ∈Z`, define the raw lift offset by
   833	`e^{2πiq_ω(ξ)}=ν_ω(z_ξ)`.  Put
   834	`X_C^∘(ξ):=X_C(ξ)-tr(rX_C(ξ))I`,
   835	`\bar q_C(ξ):=-i tr(rX_C(ξ))`, and
   836	`q_{ω,C}^∘(ξ):=q_ω(ξ)-\bar q_C(ξ) mod ℤ`.  The Hermitian registered endpoint
   837	charge is
   838	
   839	`Q_edge(ξ):=-iX_C^∘(ξ)`,
   840	
   841	and `spec Q_edge(ξ)⊂q_{ω,C}^∘(ξ)+ℤ`.  Under the phase-gauge change
   842	`V_C(exp(εξ))↦e^{iaε}V_C(exp(εξ))`, both `q_ω` and `\bar q_C` shift by `a`,
   843	while `X_C^∘`, `Q_edge`, and `q_{ω,C}^∘` are unchanged.  In the centered phase
   844	gauge `tr(rX_C)=0`, the centered and raw offsets coincide.  For `SO(3)`, that
   845	gauge gives the two offsets `1/2` and `0 mod ℤ`.  For the explicit `O(2)` pair,
   846	reflection pins the same alternatives: AKLT has `V(2π)=-I` and centered offset
   847	`1/2`, while the large-`D` product has `V(2π)=I` and centered offset `0`.  The
   848	full global group and loop are part of this statement; `U(1)` alone does not
   849	protect the offset.
   850	
   851	## D22 (twists, ordered endpoint products, and edge memory)
   852	
   853	A finite `g`-twist has two endpoints.  A single registered endpoint carries
   854	`V(g)`, while the other carries the inverse/dual multiplier; a physical
   855	endpoint observable requires (H-split) and (H-dress), meaning nonzero dressed
   856	endpoint transfer overlaps with endpoint separation taken first.  For commuting
   857	`g,h`, conjugation at one endpoint is
   858	`V(h)V(g)V(h)^{-1}=e^{i[ω(h,g)-ω(g,h)]}V(g)`; the other endpoint compensates
   859	the phase.  Ordered endpoint-soft operators obey
   860	`𝕊(h)𝕊(g)=e^{iω(h,g)}𝕊(hg)` in the registered module.
   861	
   862	Under (H-AD-edge), asymptotic total charge decomposes as
   863	`Q_tot=Q_edge+Q_bulk`.  Define
   864	`ΔQ_edge=W_+†Q_edgeW_+-W_-†Q_edgeW_-`.  “Protected” refers to the projective
   865	edge module/degeneracy, “quantised” to channel eigenvalues, and “permanent”
   866	additionally requires isolated charge-preserving edge dynamics.  Protection
   867	does not force a particular reflection matrix element to be nonzero.
   868	
   869	## D23 (exact comparison tensors and boundary Hamiltonians)
   870	
   871	In the Cartesian spin-one basis, the AKLT tensor is
   872	`A^a=σ_a/√3`, equivalently `A^0=Z/√3`,
   873	`A^{±1}=√(2/3)σ_±`.  Its `D₂` virtual operators are Pauli matrices and carry
   874	the nontrivial class.  The symmetric same-phase path is
   875	`A_b^x=a_bX`, `A_b^y=a_bY`, `A_b^z=bZ`,
   876	`a_b=√((1-b²)/2)`, `0<b<1`.
   877	
   878	The exact normalized injective `χ=2` trivial tensor is
   879	`T_t^x=[[0,t√3/2],[t/2,0]]`, `T_t^y=0`,
   880	`T_t^z=diag(√(1-t²/4),√(1-3t²/4))`, `0<t≤1`, with
   881	`θ=(1,-1,-1,1)` and `V_T=(I,Z,I,Z)` on `(e,R_x,R_y,R_z)`.
   882	At `t=1`, `r=diag(3/4,1/4)`, transfer spectrum `{1,√3/2,0,0}`, and length-two
   883	word rank four.  As `t↓0` it tends symmetrically to `|z⟩^{⊗∞}`.  The `O(2)`
   884	Lie-charge trivial comparator is the `χ=1` product tensor `P^z=1`; it is not an
   885	`SO(3)` comparator.
   886	
   887	For any such tensor `C`, set
   888	`Γ_2^C(|i⟩⊗|j⟩)=Σ_{s,t}(C^sC^t)_{ij}|s,t⟩`,
   889	`W_2^C=Γ_2^C[(Γ_2^C)†Γ_2^C]^{-1/2}`,
   890	`h_C=I-W_2^C(W_2^C)†`, and
   891	`H_{C,+}=h_{∂,C}+Σ_{x≥0}τ_x(h_C)`.
   892	For AKLT, `h_C=P^(S=2)` and `h_{∂,A}=0`.  For `T=T_1`,
   893	`h_{∂,T}=W_2^T(μ|1⟩⟨1|⊗I)(W_2^T)†`, `μ>0`; it is `D₂`-symmetric and selects
   894	the one-dimensional trivial-character edge.  For the `χ=1` `O(2)` product
   895	comparator, `h_P=I-|zz⟩⟨zz|` and `h_{∂,P}=0`.
   896	
   897	The model used by `SPT-M'-dyn` is narrowed to the Hamiltonian already supplied
   898	here, with no additional unspecified boundary term:
   899	`H_{A,+}^{dyn}:=H_{A,+}=h_{mag-edge}+Σ_{x≥1}P^{(S=2)}_{x,x+1}`.  Its exact
   900	boundary-magnon coupling is the first parent bond
   901	`h_{mag-edge}:=P^{(S=2)}_{0,1}
   902	=\tfrac16(\mathbf S_0·\mathbf S_1+2)(\mathbf S_0·\mathbf S_1+1)`.
   903	Thus the conjectured reflection matrix is the one for this specific open AKLT
   904	half-chain, not for an unnamed extra coupling.
