     1	# Ogata map: split SPT index versus charge-history memory
     2	
     3	**Status: PARTIAL/SKETCH.**  The local `refs/` tree contains no TeX source
     4	attributed to Yoshiko Ogata.  Therefore every literature attribution below is
     5	marked **UNVERIFIED-NEEDS-FETCH** with an arXiv identifier, even when official
     6	arXiv metadata/HTML was used to identify the source.  No quotation from those
     7	sources is treated as L3 ground truth.  The local side of every comparison is
     8	grounded in D26--D27 and the live claim rows named below.
     9	
    10	## 1. Executive answer
    11	
    12	1. The memory index is **strictly different** from Ogata's SPT index.  The
    13	   overlap is only at the edge: under a split/physical-edge identification,
    14	   Ogata's half-chain projective implementer is the operator-algebraic version
    15	   of the `V_C(g)` appearing in SPT-E'.  Its circle generator is the physical
    16	   counterpart of the registered `Q_edge`, not of the finite-window
    17	   `Qhat_{W,c_0}` in M-INDEX-fin.
    18	2. Stability of the SPT class under symmetric gapped automorphic equivalence
    19	   does **not** remove D27(LR).  It concerns a parameter path of ground states
    20	   and preserves a half-chain cohomology class.  D27(LR2)--(LR3) concern a
    21	   real-time excited-state TPM protocol, measurement back-action, and uniform
    22	   first-moment tightness.  None is a conclusion of index stability.
    23	3. Operator-algebraic LSM is an **admission gate only when** the proposed
    24	   memory substrate is also required to be a translation-invariant, pure split
    25	   (in particular unique gapped) state invariant under the full on-site
    26	   symmetry.  A nontrivial projective class per unit cell then forbids that
    27	   substrate.  The present kink/broken-vacuum memory setting does not satisfy
    28	   those hypotheses, so it is not forbidden.
    29	
    30	The shortest witness that the two indices are unequal is already local.  For
    31	the spin-one AKLT register, D26 has `c=1` and microscopic offset `kappa=0`,
    32	while SPT-E-AKLT has edge residue spectrum `{ -1/2,+1/2 }`.  Nevertheless
    33	M-INDEX-fin sees integer *differences* because the same finite-window offset
    34	cancels in the TPM protocol.  **Justification:** D26; claims M-INDEX-fin,
