   176	
   177	## 6. LSM constraint on calibrated memory
   178	
   179	**Literature premise (not local):** a translation-invariant chain whose
   180	on-site symmetry action carries a nontrivial projective class per unit cell
   181	admits no translation-invariant pure split state of the relevant kind, hence
   182	no unique gapped symmetric ground state in that class.
   183	**UNVERIFIED-NEEDS-FETCH arXiv:2004.06458; arXiv:1808.08740.**
   184	
   185	The consequence for this corpus is sharply scoped:
   186	
   187	| proposed substrate | LSM effect |
   188	|---|---|
   189	| Unique, gapped, translation-invariant and fully `G`-invariant split tail with nontrivial projective class per cell | Forbidden by the literature premise.  One must enlarge the unit cell until the cell class cancels, or give up at least one of translation, full symmetry, purity/split, uniqueness, or the gap. |
   190	| D9/D17 kink between a covariant broken-vacuum pair | Not forbidden by that premise: the kink state is not a translation-invariant unique fully `G`-invariant ground state. |
   191	| M-INDEX-fin on an arbitrary finite window | Unaffected: it is finite charge arithmetic under D26. |
   192	| M-IDX-density with antisymmetric tails | Independently requires `2rho in Z`; this is a positive arithmetic compatibility condition, not a no-go theorem. |
   193	
   194	In particular, D26's scalar `c` or microscopic offset `kappa` alone is not
   195	the LSM obstruction for an arbitrary `G`.  The LSM datum is the projective
   196	class of the **full physical on-site symmetry action per translation cell**.
   197	For a quotient symmetry such as the spin-rotation group, the scalar acquired
   198	by a lifted `2pi` path may help identify that class, but the identification is
   199	group-dependent and must not be inferred from D26 alone.  **Justification:**
   200	D26; claims M-IDX-density and SPT-E'; literature premise OGA-LSM at the marked
   201	unverified strength.
